    'js' => '"use strict";"use strict";//# sourceURL=customJs.js',
    'article' => [
        'intro' => <<<'TXT'
        Inflation takes Calculation based on, Start year and End year and returns Inflation rate and Inflation rate. The arithmetic is the whole method: inflation rate is worked out from calculation based on by one formula, applied once. Inflation rate comes back in percent unless you switch the unit chip beside the box.
        TXT,
        'sections' => [
            ['title' => 'How the Inflation calculator works', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>Inflation answers one question from 17 numbers, and the answer is Inflation rate, Inflation rate and Yearly rate initial.</p><p>The relationship it solves reads Annual inflation rate = (Final price ÷ Initial price)^(1 ÷ (End year − Start year)) − 1.</p><p>What comes back is Inflation rate, Inflation rate, Yearly rate initial and Yearly rate final — each answer is a box on the form, so you read it beside the numbers that produced it.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'What to enter, and what comes back', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>Fill in 17 boxes and the page has everything it needs.</p><ul><li><strong>Calculation based on</strong> — a plain number, no unit</li><li><strong>Start year</strong> — a plain number, no unit</li><li><strong>End year</strong> — a plain number, no unit</li><li><strong>Frequency</strong> — a plain number, no unit</li><li><strong>Annual inflation rate</strong> — in percent. If you do not choose consecutive years it is the average yearly inflation rate.</li><li><strong>Which was</strong> — in USD</li><li><strong>in</strong> — a plain number, no unit</li><li><strong>I must earn</strong> — in USD</li><li><strong>in</strong> — a plain number, no unit</li><li><strong>Which was</strong> — in USD</li><li><strong>in</strong> — a plain number, no unit</li><li><strong>I must earn</strong> — in USD</li><li><strong>in</strong> — a plain number, no unit</li><li><strong>Initial price</strong> — in USD</li><li><strong>Final price</strong> — in USD</li><li><strong>Price difference</strong> — in USD</li><li><strong>Cumulative inflation rate</strong> — in percent</li></ul><p>The answers come back as Inflation rate in percent, Inflation rate in percent, Yearly rate initial, Yearly rate final, Quarterly rate initial, Quarterly rate final. Those boxes are written by the page, not by you, so there is nothing to submit.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Reading the answer', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>Units are handled before the formula runs: Inflation rate offers 6 units and opens on percent, so a value typed in another one is converted on the way in and the arithmetic always happens in a single base unit.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'The formula, and what turns it round', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>Below is the arithmetic in full. Every symbol on the right of an equals sign is one of the boxes on the form.</p><ul><li><strong>Annual inflation rate</strong> = <code>(Final price ÷ Initial price)^(1 ÷ (End year − Start year)) − 1</code></li><li><strong>Final price</strong> = <code>Initial price × (Annual inflation rate + 1)^((End year − Start year))</code></li><li><strong>Initial price</strong> = <code>Final price × (Annual inflation rate + 1)^(− (End year − Start year))</code></li><li><strong>Price difference</strong> = <code>Final price − Initial price</code></li><li><strong>Cumulative inflation rate</strong> = <code>(Final price − Initial price) ÷ Initial price</code></li><li><strong>I must earn</strong> = <code>Yearly rate final × Which was ÷ Yearly rate initial</code></li></ul><p>The rearrangements, with the case each one is for — the line the page picked is the one whose left-hand side was the empty box:</p><ul><li><strong>Annual inflation rate</strong> = <code>(Final price ÷ Initial price)^(1 ÷ (End year − Start year)) − 1</code> — the form to use when Final price, Initial price, End year and Start year are the values you have.</li><li><strong>Final price</strong> = <code>Initial price × (Annual inflation rate + 1)^((End year − Start year))</code> — the form to use when Initial price, Annual inflation rate, End year and Start year are the values you have.</li><li><strong>Initial price</strong> = <code>Final price × (Annual inflation rate + 1)^(− (End year − Start year))</code> — the form to use when Final price, Annual inflation rate, End year and Start year are the values you have.</li><li><strong>Price difference</strong> = <code>Final price − Initial price</code> — the form to use when Final price and Initial price are the values you have.</li><li><strong>Initial price</strong> = <code>Final price − Price difference</code> — the form to use when Final price and Price difference are the values you have.</li><li><strong>Final price</strong> = <code>Initial price + Price difference</code> — the form to use when Initial price and Price difference are the values you have.</li><li><strong>Final price</strong> = <code>Initial price × (Cumulative inflation rate + 1)</code> — the form to use when Initial price and Cumulative inflation rate are the values you have.</li><li><strong>Initial price</strong> = <code>Final price ÷ (Cumulative inflation rate + 1)</code> — the form to use when Final price and Cumulative inflation rate are the values you have.</li></ul><p>Any one of Annual inflation rate, Final price and Initial price can be left empty: the page checks which box is blank and rearranges the relationship for it, which is why it converts as well as it calculates.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Checking an answer by hand', 'html' => '<p>A result here can always be checked: enter it in its own box, leave a different one empty, and the page should hand the first value back.</p>'],
            ['title' => 'Where the numbers usually go wrong', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <ul><li><strong>Units left where they were.</strong> The chip beside Annual inflation rate and Cumulative inflation rate is part of the input. A figure entered in millimetres while the chip reads centimetres is out by a factor of ten, and nothing on the page will warn you.</li><li><strong>The answer typed back in.</strong> A result box can be typed into, and doing so changes the question rather than the arithmetic: with Inflation rate, Inflation rate and Yearly rate initial are results filled in, the page solves for a different variable instead. Check which box is blank before reading the panel.</li><li><strong>Calculation based on left as a guess.</strong> A figure entered here because the box had to have something in it is the single most common way a correct formula produces a wrong answer. If Calculation based on is unknown, solve for it instead of estimating it — leave it blank.</li><li><strong>Start year left as a guess.</strong> A figure entered here because the box had to have something in it is the single most common way a correct formula produces a wrong answer. If Start year is unknown, solve for it instead of estimating it — leave it blank.</li><li><strong>Measurements mixed across scales.</strong> Two numbers measured in different units, or one taken at the start of a season and one at the end, are the commonest source of an answer that is internally consistent and still wrong. Convert Calculation based on to one scale before entering anything.</li></ul>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'What each box means', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>Before the arithmetic, the form itself. Each box below is named the way it is named on the page, with the unit it opens on and the error it invites if it is filled in carelessly.</p><ul><li><strong>Calculation based on</strong> takes a plain number with no unit attached. It is picked from a list, and the list matters — the options are not labels for the same number. Have it to hand before you start: Calculation based on is read the moment another box is filled, and a placeholder typed to see what happens becomes an answer.</li><li><strong>Start year</strong> takes a plain number with no unit attached. The page reads it when it works out Annual inflation rate, Final price and Initial price. Have it to hand before you start: Start year is read the moment another box is filled, and a placeholder typed to see what happens becomes an answer.</li><li><strong>End year</strong> takes a plain number with no unit attached. The page reads it when it works out Annual inflation rate, Final price and Initial price. Have it to hand before you start: End year is read the moment another box is filled, and a placeholder typed to see what happens becomes an answer.</li><li><strong>Frequency</strong> takes a plain number with no unit attached. It is picked from a list, and the list matters — the options are not labels for the same number. Have it to hand before you start: Frequency is read the moment another box is filled, and a placeholder typed to see what happens becomes an answer.</li><li><strong>Annual inflation rate</strong> is a percent quantity: the page opens it in perc and converts anything else you pick before the formula runs. If you do not choose consecutive years it is the average yearly inflation rate. The page reads it when it works out Final price and Initial price. Have it to hand before you start: Annual inflation rate is read the moment another box is filled, and a placeholder typed to see what happens becomes an answer.</li><li><strong>Which was</strong> takes a plain number with no unit attached. The page reads it when it works out Yearly rate final, I must earn and Yearly rate initial. Have it to hand before you start: Which was is read the moment another box is filled, and a placeholder typed to see what happens becomes an answer.</li><li><strong>in</strong> takes a plain number with no unit attached. It is picked from a list, and the list matters — the options are not labels for the same number. Have it to hand before you start: in is read the moment another box is filled, and a placeholder typed to see what happens becomes an answer.</li><li><strong>I must earn</strong> takes a plain number with no unit attached. The page reads it when it works out Which was, Yearly rate final and Yearly rate initial. Have it to hand before you start: I must earn is read the moment another box is filled, and a placeholder typed to see what happens becomes an answer.</li><li><strong>in</strong> takes a plain number with no unit attached. It is picked from a list, and the list matters — the options are not labels for the same number. Have it to hand before you start: in is read the moment another box is filled, and a placeholder typed to see what happens becomes an answer.</li><li><strong>Which was</strong> takes a plain number with no unit attached. The page reads it when it works out Quarterly rate initial, Quarterly rate final and I must earn. Have it to hand before you start: Which was is read the moment another box is filled, and a placeholder typed to see what happens becomes an answer.</li><li><strong>in</strong> takes a plain number with no unit attached. It is picked from a list, and the list matters — the options are not labels for the same number. Have it to hand before you start: in is read the moment another box is filled, and a placeholder typed to see what happens becomes an answer.</li><li><strong>I must earn</strong> takes a plain number with no unit attached. The page reads it when it works out Which was, Quarterly rate initial and Quarterly rate final. Have it to hand before you start: I must earn is read the moment another box is filled, and a placeholder typed to see what happens becomes an answer.</li><li><strong>in</strong> takes a plain number with no unit attached. It is picked from a list, and the list matters — the options are not labels for the same number. Have it to hand before you start: in is read the moment another box is filled, and a placeholder typed to see what happens becomes an answer.</li><li><strong>Initial price</strong> takes a plain number with no unit attached. The page reads it when it works out Annual inflation rate, Final price and Price difference. Have it to hand before you start: Initial price is read the moment another box is filled, and a placeholder typed to see what happens becomes an answer.</li><li><strong>Final price</strong> takes a plain number with no unit attached. The page reads it when it works out Annual inflation rate, Initial price and Price difference. Have it to hand before you start: Final price is read the moment another box is filled, and a placeholder typed to see what happens becomes an answer.</li><li><strong>Price difference</strong> takes a plain number with no unit attached. The page reads it when it works out Initial price and Final price. Have it to hand before you start: Price difference is read the moment another box is filled, and a placeholder typed to see what happens becomes an answer.</li><li><strong>Cumulative inflation rate</strong> is a percent quantity: the page opens it in perc and converts anything else you pick before the formula runs. The page reads it when it works out Initial price and Final price. Have it to hand before you start: Cumulative inflation rate is read the moment another box is filled, and a placeholder typed to see what happens becomes an answer.</li></ul><p>None of the boxes has to be filled in a particular order. The page reads them together and works out which single value is missing, so the last one you fill is the one it answers from.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Limits worth knowing', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <ul><li><strong>It does not scale in a straight line.</strong> Inflation rate is computed through a relationship that is not a simple proportion, so the size of an error depends on where in the range you are. The defaults are a starting point, not a scale factor.</li><li><strong>Only the listed units.</strong> The chips cover percent. A measurement in anything else has to be converted before it is typed in — the page will accept the number, but it will read it as the unit the chip shows.</li><li><strong>It is a formula, not a model of your case.</strong> Nothing here corrects for measurement error, correlation between the inputs, or conditions outside the range the relationship was written for. Those are assumptions a reader brings; the arithmetic simply does what it says.</li></ul>
